  "worked_solution": "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(2x{y}^{3})=6x{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(6x{y}^{2})=6{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6x{y}^{2}=\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''II only''.",
